\title{hvtiRimputation: Missing Value Imputation for the HVTI CORR Group}
\description{
Fills missing values in a data frame by a stated method and returns the filled data together with a row-level record of exactly which values were changed. Ports the mean-imputation behaviour of the Cleveland Clinic CORR group's SAS library -- 'PROC STANDARD' with 'REPLACE', and the 'imputsub' macro that wraps it. The package knows nothing about the warehouse, a build, or any study: the input is a data frame and the output is a data frame plus a record.
}
\details{
\strong{A data frame in, a data frame and a record out.} That is the whole
scope. The package knows nothing about the warehouse, the build, or any
study; any manifest or provenance context belongs to the caller.

\strong{Two entry points, never one.} \code{\link[=impute_mean]{impute_mean()}} fills each missing value
with its variable's mean and returns one completed dataset (\verb{PROC STANDARD ... REPLACE}, which the \code{imputsub} macro wraps). \code{impute_multiple()} will
generate \code{m} completed datasets for use with a pooling step
(\verb{PROC MI} / \code{mult_imput}); it is designed but not yet implemented.

There is deliberately no single \code{impute()} with a \verb{method=} argument. 223
studies in the corpus call single mean imputation, 326 call multiple
imputation, and 18 call both. They are different methods with different
inferential properties, and a function that quietly does one when the
caller expected the other is a worse failure than no function at all --
which is exactly what a \verb{method=} argument with a default would be. Two
functions cannot be confused by omission.

\strong{Nothing inherits a SAS default.} \verb{PROC STANDARD} with no \code{VAR} statement
processes every numeric column, and \code{impute_mean()} requires \code{vars}
anyway; \code{impute_multiple()} will require \code{m}. The reason is that the corpus
has no single answer to inherit. Five macro names exist in copies declaring
different \code{NIMPUTE} defaults and three of them straddle 1, so a plurality
default would silently convert a single-imputation call site into multiple
imputation -- a change of method under an unchanged call. Where this
package has a default it is \emph{ours}, and its documentation says so rather
than claiming parity.

\strong{What it promises about reproducing SAS.} For a call that states its
\code{NIMPUTE} outright, the method and \code{m} are facts and an R run can be checked
against the study's saved output. For a call settled only by the copy of the
macro sitting in the calling study, that is strong evidence and not proof:
which copy SAS loaded depends on the autocall path and \verb{\%include} order at
run time. A study being reproduced takes its \code{m} from that study's own saved
output, never from the macro it called -- the macro cannot tell you; the log
can. There is no general "reproduces the corpus" guarantee, and no
implementation choice here could create one.

\strong{The record is the point.} Everything returns a logical matrix parallel
to the data saying which values were filled, because a count cannot answer
\emph{was this patient's value imputed?}. See \link{hvti_imputation}.
}
